##
    Namespaces, and what they are for.

    A namespace is a place a name sits in. Two of them may each hold a Circle, and the two are
    different types that never have to know about each other. That is most of what a namespace
    is for and very nearly all of what it does.

    Everything else here follows from one rule: a bare name is read from where it is written,
    then outward, ending at the global namespace. Nearest wins.
##

##
    A using shortens names from one namespace, for one file. It brings in no code and compiles
    no extra file — it only decides which names this file can write without qualifying. Here it
    makes a bare 'Circle' mean the flat one.
##
using Geometry.Flat;

##
    The file-scoped form. Everything below belongs to Geometry, and there is no closer because
    there is nothing after it to close.

    A namespace written inside this one is a child of it rather than a second beside it, so the
    file says what it is once at the top, and then says where within itself each piece sits.
##
namespace Geometry;

##
    Declared in Geometry itself, so both namespaces below reach it with nothing written: the
    walk outward from each of them passes through here.

    This is what keeps a namespace a place to put things rather than a wall around them. A
    child reaches its parent for free; a parent reaches into a child only by saying so.
##
public abstract model Shape

    protected string name;

    public function Shape(string label)
        this.name = label;
    end function

    public virtual real function Area()
        yield 0.0;
    end function

    public override string function ToString()
        yield this.name + " of area " + Math.Round(this.Area());
    end function

end model

namespace Flat

    ##
        Written as Geometry.Flat.Circle from outside, and as Circle from anywhere already inside
        Geometry.Flat. It extends Shape, which is not declared here — that name is found by
        walking out one level.
    ##
    public model Circle extends Shape

        real radius;

        public function Circle(real radius)
            base("a flat circle");
            this.radius = radius;
        end function

        public override real function Area()
            yield Math.Pi * this.radius * this.radius;
        end function

    end model

end namespace

namespace Solid

    ##
        A second Circle, and nothing about it collides with the first. Their full names are
        Geometry.Flat.Circle and Geometry.Solid.Circle, so the compiler never has to guess, and
        a reader who needs both says which one is meant.
    ##
    public model Circle extends Shape

        real radius;

        public function Circle(real radius)
            base("a sphere");
            this.radius = radius;
        end function

        # The surface area of a sphere, which is four times the flat circle's.
        public override real function Area()
            yield 4.0 * Math.Pi * this.radius * this.radius;
        end function

    end model

end namespace

global model Program

    function Main()
        Program.WhatTheUsingDecides();
        Program.WhatQualifyingReaches();
        Program.WhatTheyShare();
        Program.WhereStandardSits();
    end function

    # ---- A using picks one namespace's names ---------------------------------------

    ##
        'Circle' with nothing in front of it is the flat one, because that is the namespace the
        file wrote a using for. Take the using away and this line stops compiling — it does not
        quietly become the other Circle.
    ##
    function WhatTheUsingDecides()
        Circle flat = new Circle(2.0);

        Console.WriteLine("a bare Circle is " + flat);
    end function

    # ---- Qualifying reaches past it -------------------------------------------------

    ##
        Writing the namespace in front reaches a type no using named. Two spellings work, and
        both mean the same type: 'Solid' is read outward from here, finds Geometry.Solid, and
        stops; 'Geometry.Solid' says the whole path and needs nothing found for it.
    ##
    function WhatQualifyingReaches()
        Solid.Circle nearby = new Solid.Circle(2.0);
        Geometry.Solid.Circle spelledOut = new Geometry.Solid.Circle(2.0);

        Console.WriteLine("Solid.Circle is " + nearby);
        Console.WriteLine("and so is Geometry.Solid.Circle: " + spelledOut);
        Console.WriteLine("the two are equal: " + (nearby == spelledOut));
    end function

    # ---- What they have in common ----------------------------------------------------

    ##
        Both Circles extend the same Shape, so both fit in one set of them. A namespace divides
        names, not types: it decides what a program has to write, and changes nothing about what
        the program then does.
    ##
    function WhatTheyShare()
        Shape[] shapes = {new Circle(1.0), new Solid.Circle(1.0)};

        Console.WriteLine("both are a Shape, so both fit in one set of them:");

        for each shape in shapes
            Console.WriteLine("  " + shape);
        end for
    end function

    # ---- The one namespace nobody declares ---------------------------------------------

    ##
        Everything the language provides sits in Standard, which is in scope in every file
        without a using. It can still be named, and naming it is how a program reaches Math when
        it has declared a Math of its own.
    ##
    function WhereStandardSits()
        Console.WriteLine("Math.Pi is          " + Math.Pi);
        Console.WriteLine("Standard.Math.Pi is " + Standard.Math.Pi);
        Console.WriteLine("the same value:     " + (Math.Pi == Standard.Math.Pi));
    end function

end model
